update public.lessons set title = 'Disability Income Insurance', description = 'Elimination periods, partial and presumptive disability, and the key policy provisions that govern a disability income claim.', duration = '10 min', content = '<p>There are two main types of disability policies. A <strong>disability income policy</strong> pays a benefit when the policyowner is disabled. All types of disability policies have a <strong>waiting period / elimination period</strong> = NO BENEFIT period. Example: the person becomes disabled in May, has a disability policy with a 2-month waiting period — they will not get a benefit in June or July, only starting in August.</p><br><ul><br><li><strong>Social Security Disability</strong>: paid by the government. To qualify, a person must be totally disabled (cannot do anything for 12 months or more, or will die). After qualifying, they need to wait a mandatory 5 months to receive money.</li><br><li><strong>Occupation/Job Disability</strong>: you or your employer buy this for your job. If you cannot do your job, you are considered totally disabled. Waiting period you choose between: 7, 30, 60, 90, or 120 days. The longer you wait, the lower your premium (wait longer = less benefit = premium cheaper; wait a little = more benefit = higher premium).</li><br></ul><br><p>Some policies provide coverage for <strong>Partial disability</strong>: after being totally disabled, the worker cannot do 1 or 2 important tasks, or cannot work full time, but is still able to work part time. Two methods of payment: <strong>Flat amount</strong> (ex: 50% of income), or <strong>Residual amount</strong> (company pays the amount income was reduced by — example: a worker normally makes $1,000/month, now can only make $700, company pays $300).</p><br><p><strong>Presumptive Disability</strong>: losses are significant enough to get full disability benefit even if they''re still able to work (ex: totally deaf, blind, mute, loss of 2 limbs / 1 arm and a leg).</p><br><p>Disability policy provisions: <strong>Recurrent provision</strong> — provides immediate benefit if the same sickness occurs within a calendar year''s period, without a new elimination period. <strong>Change of occupation</strong> — if the insured changes to a more hazardous job, benefit decreases; if to a safer job, benefit increases.</p><p>For a disability income policy, bodily injury is only classified as accidental if both the cause and the result are accidental — if either one was intentional, it does not qualify.</p><br><h3>Check Your Knowledge</h3><br><div class="lesson-quiz-item" data-correct="d"><br><p class="lesson-quiz-question">A disabled worker''s policy has a 2-month elimination period. If they became disabled in May, when does their first benefit payment begin?</p><br><div class="lesson-quiz-options"><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="a">A) May</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="b">B) June</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="c">C) July</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="d">D) August</button><br></div><br><p class="lesson-quiz-feedback" hidden></p><br><p class="lesson-quiz-explanation" hidden>With a 2-month elimination period starting in May, there''s no benefit for June or July — payments begin in August.</p><br></div><br><div class="lesson-quiz-item" data-correct="b"><br><p class="lesson-quiz-question">A worker who is totally deaf and blind qualifies for a full disability benefit even though they''re still able to work, under which provision?</p><br><div class="lesson-quiz-options"><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="a">A) Recurrent provision</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="b">B) Presumptive Disability</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="c">C) Residual disability</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="d">D) Change of occupation</button><br></div><br><p class="lesson-quiz-feedback" hidden></p><br><p class="lesson-quiz-explanation" hidden>Presumptive Disability covers losses severe enough (like total blindness or deafness) to qualify for full benefits regardless of remaining work capacity.</p><br></div><br><div class="lesson-quiz-item" data-correct="c"><br><p class="lesson-quiz-question">Under the Change of Occupation provision, if an insured switches to a MORE hazardous job, what happens to their disability benefit?</p><br><div class="lesson-quiz-options"><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="a">A) It increases</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="b">B) It stays the same</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="c">C) It decreases</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="d">D) The policy is cancelled</button><br></div><br><p class="lesson-quiz-feedback" hidden></p><br><p class="lesson-quiz-explanation" hidden>Moving to a more hazardous job decreases the benefit; moving to a safer job increases it.</p><br></div><br>', position = 7 where module_id = '5b2b91d3-1ac3-4ea8-b29a-c97281e05ef2' and title = 'Disability Income Insurance and Social Security';
insert into public.lessons (id, module_id, title, description, duration, type, content, position)
select gen_random_uuid(), '5b2b91d3-1ac3-4ea8-b29a-c97281e05ef2', 'Social Security Benefits', 'Retirement, disability, and survivor benefits under Social Security, and the fully-insured vs. currently-insured status that determines eligibility.', '10 min', 'text', '<p><strong>Social Security Benefits</strong>: Retirement, Disability, Medicare, Death benefit. Retirement ages: Normal/full retirement age is 65. Early retirement is 62. Survivor spouse retirement age is 60. To qualify to receive a benefit, a senior needs FULLY INSURED status — 40 quarters or credits, from working and paying tax for 10 years or more.</p><br><p>If a person dies with at least 6 quarters out of the last 13 quarters, they''re considered <strong>CURRENTLY insured</strong>. The surviving spouse can then receive money as early as 60 — or now, if caring for children younger than 16. If there''s no spouse, the worker''s parent (62 and over) can get the money if they were dependent on the deceased''s tax return, or the children under 19 and going to school full time.</p><br><h3>Check Your Knowledge</h3><br><div class="lesson-quiz-item" data-correct="d"><br><p class="lesson-quiz-question">A worker is entitled to Social Security disability benefits if all of the following are true, EXCEPT for:</p><br><div class="lesson-quiz-options"><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="a">A) worker is under age 65</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="b">B) worker is insured for disability benefits</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="c">C) disability is expected to continue for 12 months or result in death</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="d">D) worker cannot perform his or her current job, but is actively seeking other employment</button><br></div><br><p class="lesson-quiz-feedback" hidden></p><br><p class="lesson-quiz-explanation" hidden>Social Security disability requires the inability to do any substantial gainful work, not just the worker''s current job — someone able to seek other employment doesn''t meet that bar.</p><br></div><br><div class="lesson-quiz-item" data-correct="b"><br><p class="lesson-quiz-question">Which statement is NOT true regarding Social Security benefits?</p><br><div class="lesson-quiz-options"><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="a">A) Benefit eligibility is based upon fully insured status</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="b">B) Benefits are designed to replace the entire amount of the worker''s earnings</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="c">C) Disability is expected to continue for 12 months or result in death</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="d">D) Worker must be totally and permanently disabled for at least 5 months and unable to work again to be eligible for benefits</button><br></div><br><p class="lesson-quiz-feedback" hidden></p><br><p class="lesson-quiz-explanation" hidden>Social Security is only designed to replace a portion of pre-retirement/disability income, not the entire amount a worker earned.</p><br></div><br><div class="lesson-quiz-item" data-correct="c"><br><p class="lesson-quiz-question">How long is a person expected to be disabled in order to receive Social Security disability benefits?</p><br><div class="lesson-quiz-options"><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="a">A) 4 months</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="b">B) 6 months</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="c">C) 12 months</button><br><button type="button" class="lesson-quiz-option" data-letter="d">D) 18 months</button><br></div><br><p class="lesson-quiz-feedback" hidden></p><br><p class="lesson-quiz-explanation" hidden>To qualify, the disability must be expected to last at least 12 months, or result in death.</p><br></div><br>', 8
